# DURABLE REPRO — the `&`-in-an-OWNING-POSITION class, costume 7 of 7:
# a RETURN VALUE (`return &c.fd` from an `int` function).
#
# ⚠ THIS COSTUME DIVERGES BETWEEN THE TWO PRODUCTION BACKENDS — the only row of
# the class that does.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg):
#   * `gg check` is CLEAN on both backends.
#   * C:    accepts (with a cc pointer-to-integer warning) and prints a RAW
#           STACK ADDRESS — 281474879431680 (ASLR-dependent; the stable fact is
#           that it is a pointer, not 10).
#   * LLVM: HARD BUILD FAILURE in `llc` —
#             error: '%v4' defined with type 'ptr' but expected 'i64'
#             ret i64 %v4
#           i.e. no binary is produced at all.
# A `gg check`-clean program that miscompiles on one backend and fails to
# assemble on the other is the sharpest possible statement that the rejection
# belongs at check time.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef, measured this session): OUT OF SUBSET — "expression
# `unsupported` is outside the phase-0 subset". Its in-subset siblings (ctor,
# push, enum, array literal, tuple literal) all REJECT in ggdef, so this row is
# pinned against the class rule rather than a third implementation; recorded
# explicitly per Core #9.
#
# INTENDED: REJECTED at `gg check`. A `return` is an ownership boundary (the
# value escapes the frame), and the standing owner ruling on value-position `&`
# — recorded at `security.rs::retborrow_valuepos_amp_return_known_unsafe` —
# already says a value-position `&` must be rejected at check time and covers
# `return &v` explicitly. This is that ruling's by-value-projection sibling.
#
# The `fd` seed is COMPUTED, not a literal.
#
# See `sound_amp_owning_position_ctor.gg` for the full sibling list and the
# ruling this class rests on.
struct Counter:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

int leak_field(Counter c):
    return &c.fd

void main():
    Counter c = Counter(seed())
    print(leak_field(c))
